The actual fix was one command:


git pull --rebase
That's it. The error you hit was Git refusing to pull until you told it how to reconcile divergent branches (your 1 local commit vs. 10 remote commits). --rebase replays your local commit on top of the remote tip, giving a linear history.

Full sequence I'd run from a fresh shell:


cd /root/vfls/vLearn2

# 1. Inspect divergence first
git status -sb                       # shows "[ahead N, behind M]"
git log --oneline origin/dev..HEAD   # your local commits not on remote
git log --oneline HEAD..origin/dev   # remote commits you don't have

# 2. Pull with explicit strategy
git pull --rebase                    # rebase: linear history (recommended for dev/feature branches)
# OR
git pull --no-rebase                 # merge: creates a merge commit
# OR
git pull --ff-only                   # fast-forward only (fails if you have local commits)

# 3. Verify
git status -sb
git log --oneline -5
To make --rebase the permanent default so you can just type git pull next time:


git config --global pull.rebase true   # affects every repo on this machine
# OR
git config pull.rebase true            # affects only this repo
If a rebase ever hits a conflict (this one didn't, but for the file):


# fix conflicts in the listed files, then:
git add <the-files>
git rebase --continue
# or to abort and go back to where you were:
git rebase --abort